---
import { APPLIANCE_SOURCE } from '../data/appliances';
import { ELECTRICITY, HISTORY, stateRow } from '../data/electricity';
import { HEATING, heatingRow } from '../data/heating';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { applianceEnergy, cost } from '../lib/appliance';
import { analyzeBills } from '../lib/energy';
import { cents, dollars, monthLabel } from '../lib/format';
import { breakEvenElectricity, costAt, effectiveHspf, perTherm } from '../lib/heating';
import { blend } from '../lib/rate';

const month = monthLabel(ELECTRICITY.monthly.period);
const longDate = (d: string | undefined) =>
  d
    ? new Date(`${d}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
        month: 'long',
        day: 'numeric',
        year: 'numeric',
        timeZone: 'UTC',
      })
    : '–';

// Worked examples, computed by the same functions the calculators use.
const pa = stateRow('PA')!;
const heater = applianceEnergy({
  watts: 1500,
  hoursPerDay: 8,
  dutyCycle: 1,
  daysPerWeek: 7,
  monthsPerYear: 5,
  standbyWatts: 0,
});
const bills = analyzeBills(
  { kwh: 720, total: 131.4, days: 29, fixed: 11 },
  { kwh: 1010, total: 214.9, days: 33, fixed: 14 },
);
const partOf = (id: string) => bills.parts.find((p) => p.id === id)?.amount ?? 0;
const mn = heatingRow('MN')!;
const hpRated = costAt('heat-pump', 60, 15, 7.8);
const hpCold = costAt('heat-pump', 60, 15, effectiveHspf(7.8, 0.15, 0.1));
const repo = 'https://github.com/cheems-ai/wattsplain';
---

<Base
  title="Data & methodology"
  description="Every data source with its release and check dates, the formulas behind each calculator with worked examples you can reproduce, and the limits of the estimates."
  crumbs={[['Data & methodology', '/methodology/']]}
>
  <h1>Data & methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Where every number comes from, how each calculator works, and what the estimates cannot tell
    you. The worked examples below are computed by the same code the calculators run.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Data</th>
          <th>Period</th>
          <th>Unit</th>
          <th class="hide-sm">Released / checked</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={ELECTRICITY.monthly.source}>EIA Electric Power Monthly, Table 5.6.A</a>:
            average residential price by state
          </th>
          <td>{`${month} (preliminary) and ${monthLabel(ELECTRICITY.monthly.priorPeriod)}`}</td>
          <td>¢/kWh</td>
          <td class="hide-sm">
            {`Released ${longDate(ELECTRICITY.monthly.released)}; next ${longDate(ELECTRICITY.monthly.nextRelease)}; checked ${ELECTRICITY.fetchedAt}`}
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={ELECTRICITY.annual.source}>EIA Table 5A</a>: average monthly use and bill by
            state
          </th>
          <td>{ELECTRICITY.annual.year}</td>
          <td>kWh, $</td>
          <td class="hide-sm">{`Checked ${ELECTRICITY.fetchedAt}`}</td>
        </tr>
        {HISTORY && (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              <a href={HISTORY.source}>EIA Open Data API, retail sales</a>: monthly residential
              price by state
            </th>
            <td>{`${monthLabel(HISTORY.periods[0]!)} – ${monthLabel(HISTORY.periods.at(-1)!)}`}</td>
            <td>¢/kWh</td>
            <td class="hide-sm">{`Checked ${ELECTRICITY.fetchedAt}`}</td>
          </tr>
        )}
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={HEATING.gas.source}>EIA Natural Gas Monthly</a>: residential price by state
          </th>
          <td>{`12 months, ${monthLabel(HEATING.gas.from)} – ${monthLabel(HEATING.gas.period)}`}</td>
          <td>$/Mcf (shown per therm)</td>
          <td class="hide-sm">{`Checked ${HEATING.fetchedAt}`}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={HEATING.propane.source}>EIA State Heating Oil and Propane Program</a>
          </th>
          <td>{`Week of ${HEATING.propane.week}`}</td>
          <td>$/gal</td>
          <td class="hide-sm">{`Checked ${HEATING.fetchedAt}`}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={HEATING.recs.source}>EIA RECS {HEATING.recs.survey}, Table CE8.15.M</a>: gas
            used for space heating per gas-heated home
          </th>
          <td>{HEATING.recs.survey}</td>
          <td>million Btu</td>
          <td class="hide-sm">Survey published 2023</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={HEATING.area.source}>EIA RECS 2020, Table HC10.9</a>: average heated floor area
          </th>
          <td>2020</td>
          <td>sq ft</td>
          <td class="hide-sm">Survey published 2023</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <a href={APPLIANCE_SOURCE.url}>{APPLIANCE_SOURCE.title}</a>
          </th>
          <td>2020</td>
          <td>watts</td>
          <td class="hide-sm">Typical nameplate ranges</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How the data is updated</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A job checks EIA every day. New electricity data is published only after it passes checks:
      every state and DC present, no state twice, prices between 3¢ and 100¢, no state moving more
      than 60% in a year, no gap in the monthly history, and never older than what is already
      published. Heating fuel prices must cover every state, fall in a plausible range and never be
      older than the published week or month. If a check fails, nothing is replaced and the site
      keeps the last verified data.
    </li>
    <li>
      “Latest” on this site means the latest month EIA has published, with EIA's release date. EIA
      revises recent months; we take the revised figures when they appear.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every change to the data files is a public commit:{' '}
      <a href={`${repo}/commits/main/src/data/electricity.json`}>electricity price history</a> ·{' '}
      <a href={`${repo}/commits/main/src/data/heating.json`}>heating fuel price history</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Which electricity price</h2>
  <p>
    EIA's state figure is total residential revenue ÷ kWh sold, so fixed monthly charges are spread
    over every kWh. It is a fair yardstick for a state, but the price of one more kWh on your own
    plan can be higher or lower (tiers, time-of-use, fixed charges). Every calculator therefore lets
    you choose:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the state average (EIA);</li>
    <li>
      your own price, in ¢ or $ per kWh (a value under 1 in ¢ mode is read as dollars, and we say
      so);
    </li>
    <li>
      a price from your bill: (bill total − fixed monthly charges) ÷ kWh. Taxes and per-kWh riders
      stay in, because they grow with use;
    </li>
    <li>
      {`time-of-use: peak price × peak share + off-peak price × (1 − peak share). Example: 30% at 32¢ and 70% at 12¢ = ${cents(blend(32, 12, 0.3))}.`}
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Appliance cost</h2>
  <p>
    kWh a year = watts × running share × hours a day × days used a year ÷ 1,000 + standby watts ×
    the other hours of the year ÷ 1,000. Days used a year = 365 × days a week ÷ 7 × months a year ÷
    12. Cost = kWh × price. “Per month” is a 30-day month in which the appliance is used.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    {`Example: a 1,500 W heater, 8 hours a day, every day for 5 months: ${heater.daysPerYear.toFixed(1)} days, ${Math.round(heater.kwhPerYear).toLocaleString('en-US')} kWh a year; at Pennsylvania's ${cents(pa.price)} that is ${dollars(cost(heater.kwhPerYear, pa.price), 0)} a year and ${dollars(cost(heater.kwhPerMonthInUse, pa.price))} in a month it is used.`}
  </p>
  <p>
    Thermostat appliances (heaters) are counted at full power for every hour entered unless you
    lower the running share, so the default is an upper bound. With the EnergyGuide figure, we use
    the label's kWh a year directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Bill checker</h2>
  <p>
    Energy charges = total − fixed charges − taxes and fees + credits. Energy price per kWh = energy
    charges ÷ kWh. The change in energy charges is split with the Shapley rule into a price part,
    (p₁ − p₀) × (kWh₀ + kWh₁) ÷ 2, and a usage part, (kWh₁ − kWh₀) × (p₀ + p₁) ÷ 2, which add up
    exactly. With both billing periods, kWh = days × kWh per day is split the same way into a
    period-length part and a daily-use part. Fixed charges, taxes and credits are their own parts.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    {`Example: 720 kWh, $131.40, 29 days, $11 fixed → 1,010 kWh, $214.90, 33 days, $14 fixed: daily use ${dollars(partOf('daily-use'))}, period length ${dollars(partOf('days'))}, price per kWh ${dollars(partOf('price'))}, fixed ${dollars(partOf('fixed'))}; total ${dollars(bills.change)}.`}
  </p>
  <p>
    These are arithmetic parts, not proven causes: a higher price per kWh can be a rate change, a
    higher tier or more peak-hour use. The{' '}
    <a href="/tools/electric-bill-diagnosis/">bill diagnosis</a> labels each possible cause as shown
    by the numbers, likely from your answers, or worth checking.
  </p>

  <h2>Heating cost</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Heat a typical home needs (million Btu) = RECS gas used for space heating × 85% (the assumed
      efficiency of the furnaces in that survey). Hawaii, where EIA withholds the figure, uses the
      Pacific division.
    </li>
    <li>
      With a floor area, heat is scaled by your area ÷ the typical gas-heated home's area. RECS
      gives heated area by census division for all homes and nationally for gas-heated homes (1,837
      vs 1,614 sq ft), so the typical gas-heated area = division area × 1,837 ÷ 1,614. The
      insulation choice is a ±20% sensitivity step, not a measured factor.
    </li>
    <li>
      Fuel needed = heat ÷ (heat content × efficiency). Heat content (EIA): 3,412 Btu/kWh, 1,036 Btu
      per cubic foot of gas, 91,452 Btu/gal propane, 138,500 Btu/gal heating oil. Heat pump seasonal
      COP = HSPF2 ÷ 3.412.
    </li>
    <li>
      {`Cold weather and backup heat: COP × (1 − derate), then 1 ÷ ((1 − backup share) ÷ COP + backup share). Example at 15¢/kWh and 60 million Btu: HSPF2 7.8 as rated costs ${dollars(hpRated, 0)} a year; 15% below rating with 10% strip heat costs ${dollars(hpCold, 0)}.`}
    </li>
    <li>
      {`Break-even electricity price vs gas = gas $ per million Btu delivered × 3,412 × COP ÷ 10⁶. At Minnesota's gas price of ${dollars(perTherm(mn.prices.gas))}/therm, a 7.8 HSPF2 heat pump beats a 95% furnace below ${cents(breakEvenElectricity(mn.prices.gas, 0.95, 7.8))}/kWh.`}
    </li>
    <li>
      Long-term total (only with costs you enter) = installation + years × (running + upkeep + fixed
      charges such as a gas customer charge). No discounting, rebates or future price changes.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Savings planner</h2>
  <p>
    Your monthly kWh × 12 is the whole household. The appliances and electric heating in the first
    scenario are part of it; the remainder (“everything else”) is held the same in every scenario,
    so differences come only from what you change. Each scenario's cost = its loads + its heating +
    everything else at that scenario's price + fixed charges. The range shown moves every price 20%
    down and up.
  </p>

  <h2>State pages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      “Same use at recent prices” = the latest annual average use × the average monthly price over
      the last 12 months. It mixes two periods and is an estimate, not an actual bill.
    </li>
    <li>
      Neighbouring states are those sharing a land border (Alaska and Hawaii list their census
      division).
    </li>
    <li>Year-to-date figures are simple averages of the monthly prices.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <p>
    All results are estimates for planning, not quotes or guaranteed savings. They do not know your
    utility's exact tariff, the weather in a given year, or the condition of your equipment. Your
    utility bill is the final word. Spot a mistake? <a href="/contact/">Tell us</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
